		<td class="row2" width="100%" height="28" valign="top"><table width="100%" border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0">
			<tr>
				<td width="100%"><a href="viewtopic.php?p=8488&amp;sid=53763467292da2c86749993a0a10cf1c#8488"><img src="templates/Chronicles/images/icon_minipost.gif" width="12" height="9" alt="Post" title="Post" border="0" /></a><span class="postdetails">Posted: Tue Nov 12, 2002 5:30 pm<span class="gen">&nbsp;</span>&nbsp; &nbsp;Post subject: </span></td>
				<td valign="top" align="right" nowrap="nowrap"><a href="posting.php?mode=quote&amp;p=8488&amp;sid=53763467292da2c86749993a0a10cf1c"><img src="templates/Chronicles/images/lang_english/icon_quote.gif" alt="Reply with quote" title="Reply with quote" border="0" /></a>   </td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><hr /></td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">Morani clears her throat, straightens her posture and adopts a body language and tone of voice that mimics the wine merchants from Shadow Haven. Just a little too over zealous and with a smile in her eyes, its easy to tell she’s playing.
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
“The proper wine depends on how the meat is prepared. Lion meat can have a very delicate flavor and you wouldn’t want to overpower it, on the other hand, you don’t want the wine to be lost either. Lamaridine is right; as a common rule, a good red wine will usually compliment the meat of a large animal. If the lion meat is done as fillets or steaks, pretty much any of the reds (gypsy wine might be too strong though) or even a Feydwar Port can be complimentary. If beer braised or prepared in cream, you’ll want to stick to a lighter body and crisper wine to compliment the subtler flavors; Elven wine for the former and that or even a white for the latter. In a patty melt, the flavor will be much more robust ; that would be the time for a Gypsy wine or even a red port. Lastly, a Halas meat pie would require something bold enough to withstand the richness but full bodied enough to encourage a slow enjoyment of the meal. I would recommend either a Red or Ro port. Alternately, a wine for the pie could be skipped in favor of an after dinner brandy to cleans the palate and warm the digestion. All in all, I would suggest avoiding a fish wine unless it is used as a transition from a seafood appetizer.”
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
 
<br />

<br />
 
<br />
Morani
<br />

<br />
Wanderer of Tunare,
<br />

<br />
Protector of The Mother's children.<i></i><br />_________________<br />Morani 
<br />
Wanderer of Tunare, 
<br />
Protector of The Mother's children.</span><span class="gentblsmall"></span></td>
