				<td valign="top" align="right" nowrap="nowrap"><a href="posting.php?mode=quote&amp;p=8490&amp;sid=53763467292da2c86749993a0a10cf1c"><img src="templates/Chronicles/images/lang_english/icon_quote.gif" alt="Reply with quote" title="Reply with quote" border="0" /></a>   </td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><hr /></td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">Hmm... well, what do you prefer? I can prepare lion meat in a number of ways. In a cream sauce, as a steak, or even beer braised. Any of those would be suitable for an evening repast. I hesistate on the pies or sandwiches, as they're probably more befitting an afternoon meal. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
Morani, what do you think would go best with wurm meat? I've gathered a bit from my stay in the Great Divide. I think it'll come into a stronger taste, but the only thing I can think that might be close to something we've already tasted is something like a bolder snake meat. Definitely red, not much marbling, and has a muskier, not necessarily gamey scent than the meats I'm used to preparing, if you know what I mean. I think I may be able to make a good steak out of it, but I'd hesitate to use it with many other recipes as I believe the meat would be too overpowering of a taste. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
(ooc- I don't think there's anything else you can make out of wurm meat, and that's a good a reason as any to explain it away   ) 
